relationship: RO:0002176 UBERON:0001728 ! nasopharynx
property_value: foaf:depiction "http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Gray855.png" xsd:anyURI
property_value: UBPROP:0000001 "One of the paired openings from the nasal cavities into the pharynx. [Bemis_WE, Functional_Anatomy_of_the_Vertebrates:_An_Evolutionary_Perspective, Glossary_G-6, Grande_L, Liem_KF, Third_Edition_(2001)_Orlando_Fla.:_Harcourt_College_Publishers, Walker_WF][VHOG]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-09-17", external_class="VHOG:0000545", ontology="VHOG", source="http://bgee.unil.ch/"}
property_value: UBPROP:0000001 "Opening between the nasal cavity and the nasopharynx.[AAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-06-20", external_class="AAO:0000093", ontology="AAO", source="AAO:LAP"}
property_value: UBPROP:0000003 "The choana, a unique 'internal nostril' opening from the nasal sac into the roof of the mouth, is a key part of the tetrapod (land vertebrate) respiratory system. It was the first component of the tetrapod body plan to evolve, well before the origin of limbs, and is therefore crucial to our understanding of the beginning of the fish-tetrapod transition. (...) Here we present new material of Kenichthys, a 395-million-year-old fossil fish from China, that provides direct evidence for the origin of the choana and establishes its homology: it is indeed a displaced posterior external nostril that, during a brief transitional stage illustrated by Kenichthys, separated the maxilla from the premaxilla.[well established][VHOG]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-09-17", external_class="VHOG:0000545", ontology="VHOG", source="DOI:10.1038/nature02843 Zhu M, Ahlberg PE, The origin of the internal nostril in tetrapodes. Nature (2004)", source="http://bgee.unil.ch/"}
property_value: UBPROP:0000008 "Fish don't have choanae, instead they have two pairs of external nostrils" xsd:string {source="WP"}
property_value: UBPROP:0000008 "In addition to tetrapods, the lungfish has internal nostrils too. In tetrapods the exhalant nostril (choana) empties into the buccal cavity." xsd:string

[Term]
id: UBERON:0004772
name: eyelid tarsus
def: "The tarsi (tarsal plates) are two comparatively thick, elongated plates of dense connective tissue, about 2.5 cm. in length; one is found in each eyelid, and contributes to its form and support. They directly abut the lid margins." [Wikipedia:Tarsus_(eyelids)]
subset: organ_slim
synonym: "eyelid tarsi" RELATED OMO:0003004 []
synonym: "tarsal plate" EXACT [FMA:59086]
synonym: "tarsal plate of eyelid" EXACT [FMA:59086]
synonym: "tarsi" RELATED OMO:0003004 []
synonym: "tarsus palpebralis" EXACT [PMID:16496288]
xref: FMA:59086
xref: MA:0000270
xref: NCIT:C33736
xref: SCTID:362529005
xref: UMLS:C0459586 {source="ncithesaurus:Tarsal_Plate"}
xref: Wikipedia:Tarsus_(eyelids)
is_a: UBERON:0000094 {source="FMA"} ! membrane organ
relationship: BFO:0000050 UBERON:0003581 {source="cjm"} ! eyelid connective tissue
relationship: BFO:0000051 CHEBI:18085
property_value: UBPROP:0000008 "In most taxa (birds, mammals, lizards), the tarsal plate is described as a dense, fibrous connective tissue, possibly including cartilage, present within one or both of the upper and lower eyelids (Gau- thier et al., 1988; Rieppel, 2000). In humans, the tarsal plate of the upper eyelid is composed of collagens types I, III, and V, as well as glycosaminogly- cans (chondroitin sulphate 4 and 6), aggrecan, and cartilage oligomeric matrix proteins but lacks collagen type II as well as chondrocytes (Milz et al., 2005). Thus, for humans, the upper tarsal plate represents neither a truly fibrous nor a truly cartilagi- nous element but instead one that is composed of a unique transitional tissue (Milz et al., 2005). In many birds, lizards, and Sphenodon (the tuatara), the upper eyelid has lim- ited mobility and a putative tarsal plate is instead found within the lower eyelid (Underwood, 1970; Gau- thier et al., 1988)." xsd:string {source="PMID:16496288"}

